}

^["risk_model"]{
 hard_failures: ["editor and plan:check disagree on open_count, so the editor reports drift the CLI does not", "plugin writes a cache field the CLI also writes, racing plan:stream"]
 soft_failures: ["plan files indexed before the manifest names .agents/plans as a root, so facts appear only for open documents"]
 recoveries: ["drift diagnostics are information severity and never carry a write fix; the fix stays plan:stream or the human"]
 non_negotiables: ["the editor never edits ~#last_stream or any cache fact", "Neovim and IntelliJ get plan facts from standard LSP methods, not client chrome"]
}

^["validation"]{
 hypotheses: ["one extractor over the seed parser reproduces plan:check open_count and last_stream results on every wip.spw"]
 negative_controls: ["outline output for non-plan .spw files is unchanged", "plan:check results are unchanged"]
 demo_path: ["open a wip.spw in VS Code, Neovim and IntelliJ", "see ^cache keys in the outline", "hover ~#open_count", "edit a ?[ line and see a drift diagnostic"]
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] observe — outlineFromSource on a real wip.spw lists the section frames (card, edges, roots, stream, open, commits, done, cache), the ?[label] lines under ^open as children, and wonder blocks with their !probe; it lists no ~[N] commit rows, no ^cache keys, no ^stream entries and no ^done keys.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] observe — across the wip.spw of all 80 plan folders the count of ?-children under ^open in that outline equals ~#open_count in every file, so open_count drift can be computed from document symbols alone.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] observe — three code paths read plan facts: agent-lib.sh counts open questions by line grep, scripts/plans/plan-index.ts uses lex and parse from the seed, and packages/spw-lsp has no handler that names open_count, last_stream or next_commit.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] observe — no field named clock, timezone or tz appears in workspace-protocol.ts, workspace-authority.ts or workspace-roots.ts, while the authoring plan asks for a stamp from the manifest declared clock; plan:stream builds its stamp in agent-lib.sh.
 >>[2026-10-12 15:24] revise — Commits named per the convention in 90c58bbc: 1 landed, 6 planned, 0 dropped.
 >>[2026-10-12 17:02] decide — Creator decided 2026-10-12: the hash syntax .[name](hash): "subject" is the next slice, after release. The plan-only lexer rule comes first (plan-stream-parse-health), then the one-pass conversion (plan-tooling-design), then editor affordances (plan-plugin-integration).
 >>[2026-10-12 20:09] implement — Landed in e6c56978: commit-ref hover and the missing-hash note in plan files. Links, completion and a token type move to commit_ref_links.
 >>[2026-10-12 21:02] decide — Creator asked for completions beyond stamps and types: landing a planned commit from git log (~[land_commit_action], which takes over hash completion from ~[commit_ref_links]), plan-name completion and navigation, schema keys, and stream completion wherever a stream frame is.
 >>[2026-10-13 11:59] revise — resolved [clock_field]: e6c56978 reads a clock zone from .spw/site.spw or .spw/workspace.spw, else SPW_TZ, else local time. The IntelliJ outline is a Kotlin line reader that never asks the server (hosts/jetbrains-native-features.spw), so cross_editor_check expects it absent. Stream completion here extends the same handler vscode-authoring-probe-loop landed as stream_stamp_completion; coordinate before stream_completion_everywhere.
 >>[2026-10-13 15:06] decide — The owner escalated git-hash support in VS Code on 2026-10-13; it becomes this plan's first slice and a now row in the plan index priority. commit_ref_links splits into commit_ref_token_type, commit_ref_document_link, and commit_view_vscode; commit_ref_beyond_plans and commit_ref_vscode_test are added, with ?[prose_hashes] deciding how far hashes outside the landed form reach. Git calls stay in commit-refs.ts for this slice; local-numbering-vcs lsp_adapter moves them later.
 >>[2026-10-13 15:33] decide — resolved [prose_hashes]: the owner chose to catch more, with a maintainable resolution strategy. Any 7 to 40 hex run with a digit and a letter that git confirms as a commit hovers and links wherever it stands and takes no color; landed refs keep their token and missing-hash note.
 >>[2026-10-13 15:33] implement — Landed a80a5df7: COMMIT_REF_STRATEGY and READERS in commit-refs.ts hold the rule as data, git cat-file --batch-check confirms many hashes in one process, links name full hashes in spw-commit URIs, and each landed hash is one commitRef token. Landed 00c6a56e: VS Code opens a commit link as a read-only diff document, and Spw: Open Commit Changes opens the multi-file changes view through the Git extension, falling back to the document. No automated test drives the changes view; commit_ref_vscode_test is next.
 >>[2026-10-13 17:16] observe — Neovim reached parity with the VS Code commit slice in bdd48943: gf, :SpwCommit, :SpwCommitFiles, and the commitRef highlight, all from the same server links and token. commit_ref_vscode_test remains VS Code only.
 >>[2026-10-13 18:13] decide — The owner asked for a tokenization for non-commit hashlikes after ~<1>[begin_syntax](ebcde35) in a scratch file registered as nothing. This revises the rule that cited hashes take no color: the strategy now names a token by form, never by git (commitRef for a landed ref, hashlike for every other hash-shaped run), git still decides links, and a hover over a hashlike git does not hold says it is not a commit here. Landed in 1ea9ac14 for both editors.
}

^["open"]{
 ?[extractor_home]: "Where does the one plan-fact extractor live: packages/spw-seed (portable, parse-based, LSP and CLI both import it), or scripts/plans/plan-index.ts exported to the LSP? Decides ~[shared_extractor] and whether agent-lib.sh keeps its line grep."
 ?[drift_severity]: "Should open_count and last_stream drift be information or warning, given plan:stream and spw refresh also write them and a stale cache is already reported by plan:check?"
 ?[land_ranking]: "Which commits does landing offer first: HEAD, commits whose sigil and scope match the planned line, or a ranking by subject words? A site commit lands in another repository; does the action read that repository's log?"
 ?[done_in_outline]: "Do ^done entries (~#resolved(label), ~#dropped(name)) appear as outline children, as hover on ?[label] mentions, or both? Decides ~[plan_fact_hover] and what Neovim sees."
}

^["commits"]{
 .[stage_plan](7b4d7b5d): ".[plans] — open four plans: stream parse health, plan tooling, plan plugin integration, and a lukewarm backlog"
 ~[plan_facts_table]: ".[plans] — write references/plan-facts.spw: each plan fact with source frame, LSP product, owner plan, and the hosts that render that product (hosts/host-parity-matrix.spw)"
 ~[shared_extractor]: "&[seed] — one plan-fact extractor; plan-index.ts calls it, with a test that it matches plan:check on every wip.spw"
 ~[outline_plan_facts]: "&[lsp] — outline lists ^cache keys, ~[name] and .[name] commit rows and ^done keys as children of their frames"
 ~[plan_fact_hover]: "&[lsp] — hover on ^cache and card fields and on ~#resolved(label) shows the value, its derivation and the stream stamp"
 ~[drift_diagnostics]: "&[lsp] — drift diagnostics for open_count, last_stream and next_commit against the file, information severity, no write fix"
 .[commit_ref_affordances](e6c56978): "&[lsp] — in plan files a commit-ref hash hovers with its subject, date and files, and a hash git does not have gets an information note"
 .[commit_ref_token_type](a80a5df7): "&[lsp] — one hash resolution strategy: landed refs take a commitRef token, and every hash git confirms hovers and links to its commit"
 .[commit_ref_document_link](a80a5df7): "&[lsp] — one hash resolution strategy: landed refs take a commitRef token, and every hash git confirms hovers and links to its commit"
 .[commit_view_vscode](00c6a56e): "&[vscode] — commit links open inside VS Code: a read-only diff document, and the changes view through the Git extension"
 .[commit_ref_beyond_plans](a80a5df7): "&[lsp] — one hash resolution strategy: landed refs take a commitRef token, and every hash git confirms hovers and links to its commit"
 ~[commit_ref_vscode_test]: "![vscode] — extension test on a fixture plan with a known commit: hover, token, link, open, and completion; an unknown hash gets the note and no link"
 ~[land_commit_action]: "&[lsp] — on a ~[name] line in ^[\"commits\"], completion after ]( and a code action offer recent commits with their subjects and rewrite the line to .[name](hash): \"subject\"; the same move as plan-tooling-design ~[settle_verb] --ship"
 ~[plan_name_completion]: "&[lsp] — ~#next_commit: \" offers the planned ~[name]s, ~#resolved( the open ?[label]s, ~#dropped( the planned names, and a stream entry body this file's names"
 ~[plan_name_navigation]: "&[lsp] — go-to-definition and references for plan names: ~#next_commit, ~#resolved(label), a mention in an entry and another plan's ~\"../x/wip.spw#name\" reach the ~[name], .[name] or ?[label] line (needs directive-lattice ~[plan_name_fragments])"
 ~[schema_key_completion]: "&[lsp] — inside ^[\"cache\"]{ and the card, the keys .agents/plans/_schema/wip.spw declares and the file lacks; phase: # offers the phases; the card glyph offers the lanes"
 ~[stream_completion_everywhere]: "&[lsp] — after plan-stream-parse-health ~[entry_line_all_dialects]: >>[ completes the stamp in any ^[\"stream\"] frame and the next number in an ordinal stream; isPlanStreamFile stops gating it"
 ~[cross_editor_check]: "![plans] — Neovim and IntelliJ receive ~[plan_fact_hover] and ~[drift_diagnostics] with no client code; the IntelliJ outline is expected absent, since its structure view is a Kotlin line reader that never asks the server (hosts/jetbrains-native-features.spw)"
}

